          "reasoning_chars": 0,
          "content": "</ifm|think_faster>Heat pumps move heat rather than generate it, transferring warmth from one place to another with a reversing valve and refrigerant. In cooling mode, the outdoor unit pulls warm air off your house, compresses and heats the refrigerant, which then releases its heat indoors through the air handler or furnace coil while the refrigerant re-expands, drawing in fresh indoor air. In heating mode, the reversing valve flips the cycle: the outdoor unit absorbs heat from the outside air (even at low temperatures, since heat energy exists in the air molecules), and the compressed refrigerant releases that collected warmth inside, raising your thermostat. Because the system moves an amount of heat that's typically 2\u20134 times larger than the electrical energy it consumes, it runs far more efficiently than resistance heat.",
          "reasoning_content": "",
          "tool_calls": null
        },
